A||||7||Tom88gt||opinions on "motor flush"???||||5||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||01-16-2000||05:18 PM||||I've used Gunk motor flush a few times in the past and been happy with the results.  Someone told me a while back that it is basicaly the same as diesl fuel.  I've flushed engines a few times with diesl and it appears to be ok at flushing out the sludge and build up inside the motor, and a lot cheaper too.<P>Is this ok or not?<P>Also, how often should you flush your engine?  And does synthetic oil have a similar flushing effect?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Tom88gt||01-16-2000||05:24 PM||||I've also used Naptha as a fuel injection/carb clearer.  Most fuel ijector/carb cleaners don't tell you whats in the bottle.  But a couple do.  The back said Naptha.  I buy it at the hardware store for about $5 gal instead of fuel injector cleaner for $5 for a few ounces.<P>I've always believed that most automotive chemicals and cleaners are nothing more than common fluids from the local hardware store, packaged for single use and marked up way too much.<P>Any thoughts on this as well?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||01-16-2000||05:55 PM||||I agree. We have used Kerosene and diesel fuel to de-gunk engines with pretty good luck. I have not thought of using hardware store naptha for injector cleaner, but I have used the gallon of acetone for a paint stripper and paint gun soak to clean really tough paint from the gun's internals. I have also ran the hardware denatured alcohol as a race fuel in my gokart. It is ethanol with a little methanol (so folks won't drink it!) and would make more heat and power than the track purchased fuel. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||01-16-2000||08:43 PM||||Motor flush is just kerosene. The best way to keep the sludge out of your engine is to change your oil every three thousand miles. You risk bearing damage using that stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||01-17-2000||12:24 AM||||I flushed my motor with the big can of K&W without problems... My Grandfather told me that it's hard on the seals, but everyone has their own opinions.||||reg||1
Z||000005||geyser||01-17-2000||02:08 AM||||Transmission fluid will work for a minor flushing. Drain oil, add tranny fluid and run for 10-15 minutes at 1500-2000 rpm in neutral. Drain and replace filter. If the motor is really gunked up, don't flush it as you will loosen up all that sludge and gunk and circulate it throughout your engine; relocating it to places you don't want it. Best not to flush at all. Best insurance, as you are aware, is to keep the oil changed... regular intervals of 5k to 7k, unless you fall into the "severe" driving as outlined in the owners manual. Any shorter intervals and you are wasting your money.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mutfish||01-17-2000||11:08 PM||||If you really give this motor flush thing some thought,the solvent you use to flush the gunk from the internals is usually a kerosine based product.It will mix with ,and therefor dilute the oil.Even after you drain this mixture,there has to be a significant amount still floating around in the engine,such as in the lifters,oil gallies,nooks and crannies ect. This crap is then mixed with and contaminates the fresh oil......just a thought....||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tom88gt||01-19-2000||01:26 PM||||Yeah Mut, I'm always thinking about that too.  I just plan on doing another oil change in a week to help clear out the stuff.  I'll get some karossene (spelling??) and compare it to diesl.<P>Some thing that flushing the engine will cause leaks by removing gunk from seals and gaskets.  But then the gunk was just a bandaid for a tired motor which needs to be fixed, not ignored.||||reg||1
